 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Strengths:** Exact analytical distances; robust rendering with interval arithmetic bisection tracers [S1, unofficial]; supports CSG operations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Open meshes generate skirts up to `trunc_dist` past the rim [S3, unofficial]; inside-out meshes remain inside-out [S3, unofficial]; edges/corners with angles under 90 degrees may have incorrect signs due to nearest-face tie-breaking by lowest index [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Rendering CAD meshes (e.g., brake calipers, brackets) as implicit surfaces [S1, unofficial]; generating offset meshes for isosurfacing [S4].
-*   **When not to use:** Open meshes requiring precise boundary handling without skirts (use Shell mode) [S3, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot V)$ for brute-force voxelization, where $N$ is the number of triangles and $V$ is the number of voxels. Using spatial acceleration structures (AABB trees) reduces query time to $O(\log N)$ per voxel [S4].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for dense grids. Sparse structures reduce this to $O(N)$ near the surface [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Depends heavily on mesh complexity and grid resolution. GPU implementations can parallelize triangle iteration [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Robust rendering of complex CAD meshes; supports CSG operations; differentiable depth rendering via implicit function theorem [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Open meshes require special handling (skirt); sharp angles can cause sign errors; brute-force is slow for high-poly meshes [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the need to render complex CAD meshes as implicit surfaces using robust interval-arithmetic bisection tracers [S1, unofficial]. It was implemented in libraries like `isoray` to support STL files and in CGAL's `Isosurfacing_3` module for contouring mesh offsets [S4][S1, unofficial]. The technique leverages spatial acceleration structures (AABB trees) and side-of-mesh functors to efficiently compute signed distances, enabling applications like dual contouring and marching cubes on mesh-derived fields [S4].
+The method originates from the need to render explicit triangle meshes (e.g., STL files) using implicit surface rendering techniques, such as interval-arithmetic bisection [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in libraries like `isoray` for robust iso-surface rendering and CGAL's `Isosurfacing_3` module for contouring mesh offsets [S1, unofficial][S4]. The approach allows CAD meshes to be rendered with the same robustness as analytic implicit functions [S1, unofficial].
 
